# Budget ceilings — the ratchet (CONSTITUTION §4). Raise ONLY in a dedicated commit, never to
# silence a regression. Re-baseline deliberately with: scripts/budget.sh --update
#
# 2026-07-26 — SRC_LOC_MAX 1714 -> 3436, one session, one capability: reading a page that carries
# its structure in typography, geometry and colour rather than in letters. It arrived in three
# pushes (text intake; layout geometry, progress protocol and the streaming endpoint; the drawn-
# structure reader) and each was new ability, not a regression.
#
# What the earlier note in this file got wrong: it said "split instead". Splitting does not help.
# budget.sh totals every line under src/, so dividing a module raises the count. The duplication it
# named was real and has been removed — one tesseract invocation now serves both text and TSV —
# and that recovered eight lines. Reducing this figure means deleting capability, nothing else.
#
# THREE RAISES IN ONE SESSION IS NOT A PRECEDENT. The next one should be argued for in its own
# right, by someone who has read what the 3436 lines actually do.
#
# Raised by hand rather than with --update, because --update would also lift DOC_TOKENS_MAX,
# which has not moved and should stay where it is.
# 2026-07-26 — DOC_TOKENS_MAX 8600 -> 9610. Two additions, both the ceiling's purpose rather than
# a breach of it: README gained the Getting-started and Prerequisites sections a public clone needs
# (without them `./start.sh` succeeds and the first dropped screenshot fails on a missing binary),
# and BIBLE gained this session's decision register. Trimmed once before raising.
#
# 2026-07-27 — SRC_LOC_MAX 3436 -> 3831, DOC_TOKENS_MAX 9610 -> 11870. One capability and one
# document, argued in their own right as the note above demands.
#
# Source: `draft.py` (the draft a person edits, and the rules that judge it) plus `ExportPanel.tsx`
# lifted out of `App.tsx`. It exists because the studio could recognise three cards and show none
# of them — the JSON editor lived only in the branch where an export already existed. Nothing here
# duplicates the exporter: `check_deck` is the one place the target's rules are applied to JSON
# that has no Capture IR behind it, and the browser asks it rather than re-implementing it.
#
# Docs: the README now carries a worked example — one quiz with and without its answer key, with
# the real JSON and the real gate messages — because a public tool that never shows its output
# asks a reader to take it on faith. BIBLE and HANDOFF grew by this session's entries.
#
# 2026-07-27 (second) — SRC_LOC_MAX 3831 -> 3920. One capability: re-reading an emphasised row on
# its own, cropped out of the coloured box that defeated the page-wide pass. It is not a tweak —
# on a real practice assessment four of fourteen exported cards carried a mangled correct answer
# ("Therei h thi Sprint 0 in 5"), and every one of them was the tinted row, because the tint is
# what marks the answer. Thirteen of fourteen now read as clean prose. The lines bought are the
# crop, the two judgements that accept or refuse a re-read, and the widget strip that must not
# eat a real two-letter word.
#
# 2026-07-27 (third) — SRC_LOC_MAX 3920 -> 4180. `quality.py`: a verdict on recognised text,
# separate from every reader that produces it. The readers each fix one layout; this asks the
# question none of them can ask about itself — did it work? — and it is what stops the failure
# class that prompted it: four of fourteen exported cards carried a correct answer nobody could
# read, at tier source-verified, because something *was* marked and the text *was* non-empty.
# A marked answer that fails the verdict can no longer be source-verified. The lines are the two
# script tests, the furniture and letter-ratio checks, and the fold that repairs what is
# unambiguous while leaving the rest reported.
# 2026-07-27 (fourth) — SRC_LOC_MAX 4180 -> 4300. `vision.py`: a third reading of a page, from a
# local vision-OCR model over loopback ollama, plus the score that decides when it wins.
#
# Argued in its own right, as the note above demands. The reason is a measured wrong answer, not a
# preference for newer tooling: on `fixtures/images/quiz.png` tesseract drops the leading digit of
# question 2, the metals block inherits its neighbour's number, and the key row "2. A, C" binds
# elsewhere — the card exports "Iron" alone, at tier source-verified, unblocked. No post-processing
# recovers a digit that was never recognised, which is why this is a reader and not a repair.
#
# Both readings find three answerable questions, so `usable_blocks` cannot separate them. The lines
# bought are therefore two things, not one: the reader itself, and `collided_indices` — the count
# of answerable blocks claiming a number another already claimed, which is what a lost digit looks
# like from above and is the only signal that ranked the correct reading first.
#
# It buys no dependency: urllib and base64 are stdlib, and absent ollama the reader returns "" and
# the tesseract readings decide alone. A machine without a model is exactly as capable as before.
# 2026-07-27 (fifth) — SRC_LOC_MAX 4300 -> 4450. The second raise for the same capability today,
# which the note above says should not become a habit; it is here because using the reader on a
# real document found what the fixture could not.
#
# Banding (`vision.band_bounds`, `is_blank_row`, `_bands`). A 2500x28662 assessment read whole came
# back with "Select that one" where the page prints "Select all that apply" — a multiple-select
# question silently turned single-choice — plus "Definition of Reedy" and "Name of the above". The
# same model, given the same page in 1850px bands, returned all three verbatim and found all twenty
# questions where the previous reading found fourteen. Cuts land on background rows, so no text is
# transcribed twice and nothing downstream has to dedupe.
#
# `marks_correctness` and `positional_marker` are the two things using it taught. The first stops a
# sixteen-band read that could not have won: a page that marks its answers in colour carries the
# correctness a transcript cannot, so the reading that already holds the answers is not displaced —
# 103s instead of 8m26s on that document, with the same nineteen cards. The second is a crash:
# `LATIN_IDS[i]` raised IndexError on a block of thirty options, losing every other question in the
# upload.
DOC_TOKENS_MAX=11870
SRC_LOC_MAX=4450
